**11. Language Selection & i18n UI**

Language support is a visible product feature --- not just a technical
implementation detail. The language selection experience must be obvious
for Spanish-speaking users, discoverable for all users, and designed
with the reality that many field workers will use their device in
Spanish even if the app defaults to English.

**11.1 Language Detection --- First-Run Behavior**

  -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  **Scenario**       **App Behavior**
  ------------------ ----------------------------------------------------
  Device locale =    App launches in Spanish automatically --- no prompt.
  Spanish (es or     User sees onboarding wizard in Spanish.
  es-\*)             

  Device locale =    App launches in English (PT not yet supported in
  Portuguese (pt or  v1.0) with a banner: \'Portuguese coming soon. Your
  pt-BR)             app is in English for now.\'

  Device locale =    App launches in English. Language selector visible
  any other          in Profile settings.
  unsupported        
  language           

  User was invited   Invitation email is in Spanish. App onboarding
  by a               respects Accept-Language header from device.
  Spanish-speaking   
  tenant             

  User changes       Immediate effect --- all screens re-render in new
  language in        language. No restart required.
  Profile            
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------

**11.2 Language Selector Component**

The language selector appears in three locations: (1) the
signup/onboarding wizard, (2) Profile Settings, and (3) the mobile
app\'s first-run setup. It is a simple dropdown --- not buried in
settings.

> ┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐
>
> │ Language / Idioma │
>
> │ ● English │
>
> │ ○ Español │
>
> │ ○ Português (Brazilian) --- coming soon │
>
> │ ○ Kreyòl ayisyen --- coming soon │
>
> └─────────────────────────────────────────┘

Language options labeled in both English AND the native language
(\'Español\' not \'Spanish\', \'Português\' not \'Portuguese\') --- a
Spanish speaker who doesn\'t read English must be able to identify their
language.

**11.3 Bilingual Labels --- Key UI Strings**

Certain high-frequency UI elements use bilingual labels at launch ---
especially in the mobile app --- to reduce friction for Spanish speakers
even before they\'ve set their language preference:

-   Clock In / Fichar Entrada --- shown bilingually until user has set
    language preference

-   Submit Report / Enviar Reporte --- daily report submit button on
    mobile

-   Inspection FAILED / INSPECCIÓN FALLIDA --- critical safety
    notification always bilingual regardless of language setting

-   Emergency contact / Contacto de emergencia --- safety onboarding
    screen

**11.4 RTL Layout Preparation**

Arabic (v2.0+) requires right-to-left layout. The design system must be
built with RTL in mind from v1.0 to avoid a rebuild later. Key design
rules:

-   Use CSS logical properties in all components: margin-inline-start,
    padding-inline-end, border-inline-start --- never directional
    margin-left/margin-right

-   Icons that imply direction (arrows, navigation chevrons) must flip
    in RTL --- use CSS transform: scaleX(-1) or dedicated RTL icon
    variants

-   Text alignment: use start/end not left/right --- text-align: start
    aligns left in LTR, right in RTL automatically

-   Bidirectional text: user-entered content may mix Arabic and English
    --- use unicode-bidi: embed on text containers
